      "id": 1,
      "text": "Neon's name comes from the Greek word 'neon,' meaning 'new,' reflecting its status as a freshly developed town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fleming-Neon%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Fleming began in 1913 as a model city built by the Mineral Fuel Company, later part of the Elkhorn Coal Corporation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fleming-Neon%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "For decades, Neon and Fleming stayed separate municipalities, split only by the L&N Railroad tracks.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fleming-Neon%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Judge John F. formally incorporated Fleming-Neon in August 1916, ending a period of noted lawlessness.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fleming-Neon%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Before becoming Neon, the area was known as Chip, a lumber yard and storage site for yellow poplar.",
